Q: toFixed is not rounding in JavaScript A: <p>Use:</p>
<pre><code>function roundup(num, dec) {
    dec = dec || 0;
    var s = String(num);
    if(num%1)
        s = s.replace(/5$/, '6');
    return Number((+s).toFixed(dec));
 }

 var n = 35.855
 roundup(n, 2)
</code></pre>
<p>/*  returned value: (Number)
35.86
*/</p>

Q: Disable HTTP OPTIONS, TRACE, HEAD, COPY and UNLOCK methods in IIS A: <p>This worked for me but only after forcing the specific verbs to be handled by the default handler.</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;system.web&gt;
...
  &lt;httpHandlers&gt;
  ... 
    &lt;add path=&quot;*&quot; verb=&quot;OPTIONS&quot; type=&quot;System.Web.DefaultHttpHandler&quot; validate=&quot;true&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;add path=&quot;*&quot; verb=&quot;TRACE&quot; type=&quot;System.Web.DefaultHttpHandler&quot; validate=&quot;true&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;add path=&quot;*&quot; verb=&quot;HEAD&quot; type=&quot;System.Web.DefaultHttpHandler&quot; validate=&quot;true&quot;/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You still use the same configuration as you have above, but also force the verbs to be handled with the default handler and validated. Source: <a href="http://forums.asp.net/t/1311323.aspx" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://forums.asp.net/t/1311323.aspx</a></p>
<p>An easy way to test is just to deny GET and see if your site loads.</p>

Q: how to disable insecure http methods(OPTIONS,PUT,DELETE) A: <p>You need to make these settings in the web.config file.</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;system.web&gt;
...
  &lt;httpHandlers&gt;
  ... 
    &lt;add path=&quot;*&quot; verb=&quot;OPTIONS&quot; type=&quot;System.Web.DefaultHttpHandler&quot; validate=&quot;true&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;add path=&quot;*&quot; verb=&quot;TRACE&quot; type=&quot;System.Web.DefaultHttpHandler&quot; validate=&quot;true&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;add path=&quot;*&quot; verb=&quot;HEAD&quot; type=&quot;System.Web.DefaultHttpHandler&quot; validate=&quot;true&quot;/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/18585548/3870428">For more information, look at the BrutalDev's post</a></p>
